  "raw_text": "Step 1: Let the two-digit number be represented as \\(10a + b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the tens and units digits respectively.\n\nStep 2: According to the problem, the sum of the digits is 13, so we have the equation:\n\\[a + b = 13.\\]\n\nStep 3: The number with its digits reversed is \\(10b + a\\). The difference between the original number and the reversed number is 27, so we have the equation:\n\\[10a + b - (10b + a) = 27.\\]\nSimplifying this, we get:\n\\[9a - 9b = 27,\\]\nwhich simplifies further to:\n\\[a - b = 3.\\]\n\nStep 4: Now we have a system of linear equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\na + b = 13 \\\\\na - b = 3\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nStep 5: Adding these two equations together eliminates \\(b\\):\n\\[(a + b) + (a - b) = 13 + 3,\\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\\[2a = 16,\\]\nso:\n\\[a = 8.\\]\n\nStep 6: Substituting \\(a = 8\\) back into the first equation:\n\\[8 + b = 13,\\]\nso:\n\\[b = 5.\\]\n\nStep 7: Therefore, the original number is \\(10a + b = 10 \\cdot 8 + 5 = 85\\), and the reversed number is \\(10b + a = 10 \\cdot 5 + 8 = 58\\).\n\nStep 8: The sum of the original number and the reversed number is:\n\\[85 + 58 = 143.\\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{143}<|im_end|>",
